---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SwitchFilm from "../components/SwitchFilm.astro";
import { getRelease, site, repository } from "../lib/release";
const r = await getRelease();
const scenes = [
  [
    "01",
    "PROFILES",
    "Your identities.\nYour decision.",
    "Keep personal, work and reserved profiles in a single dock. No fixed account cap. No automatic rotation. Friend Priority stays exactly where you put it.",
    "/docs/add-profile",
  ],
  [
    "02",
    "USAGE",
    "Know what is known.",
    "Codex reads real app-server limits independently. Claude Code beta reads supported status-line quota after a response. Last confirmed is labelled. Missing data is N/A, never an invented allowance.",
    "/docs/providers",
  ],
  [
    "03",
    "PROVIDERS",
    "Two tools.\nDifferent rules.",
    "Codex opens the established Desktop workspace through a normal-quit handoff. Claude Code beta opens an isolated Windows terminal with its own configuration directory. One interface does not mean identical capabilities.",
    "/providers",
  ],
  [
    "04",
    "LAUNCH",
    "A folder.\nA profile.\nGo.",
    "Choose a project and an explicit provider profile. Claude opens in that folder. Codex keeps its existing workspace. Authentication remains with each provider's normal sign-in flow.",
    "/how-it-works",
  ],
  [
    "05",
    "QUICK",
    "Find your next move.",
    "Search provider, profile and project from the desktop quick launcher. Try the website demo with Ctrl + K. This page cannot control your local apps.",
    "/docs/quick-launch",
  ],
];
const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
      name: "VeyDock",
      alternateName: "VDock",
      description:
        "Free, open-source Codex account manager for Windows with real usage limits, project shortcuts and Claude Code profile support in beta.",
      operatingSystem: "Windows",
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      image: `${site}/media/veydock-social.png`,
      screenshot: `${site}/media/veydock-mixed-provider-dock.webp`,
      featureList: ["Codex profile management", "Provider-reported Codex usage limits", "Claude Code profiles (beta)", "Project shortcuts", "Streamer mode", "No fixed profile cap"],
      applicationCategory: "DeveloperApplication",
      url: site,
      downloadUrl: r.url,
      softwareVersion: r.version,
      license: `${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Mithil Katkoria" },
      offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
    },
    { "@type": "WebSite", name: "VeyDock", url: site },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="VeyDock | Free Codex Account Manager for Windows"
  description="Manage multiple Codex accounts and real usage limits on Windows. Free, MIT open source, with project shortcuts and Claude Code profiles in beta."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="universal-hero wrap">
    <div class="hero-index mono">
      <span>VEYDOCK / VDOCK</span>
      <span>FREE SOFTWARE / MIT OPEN SOURCE</span>
    </div>
    <div class="universal-heading">
      <h1>
        One dock.
        <br />
        Your AI coding
        <br />
        <span>profiles.</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="hero-side">
        <p class="eyebrow">CONTROL, WITHOUT THE CLUTTER</p>
        <p>
          A free, open-source account manager for Codex on Windows. Compare
          real usage limits, organise your profiles and choose your next session.
          Claude Code profiles are available in beta.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button" href="/download">
            Download free for Windows <span>↓</span>
          </a>
          <a class="text-link" href="#walkthrough">
            Watch the switch ▶
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="mono release-line">
          FREE DOWNLOAD / v{r.version} / X64 / MIT
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="provider-strip">
      <a href="/providers/codex">
        <span class="provider-number mono">01</span>
        <strong>Codex</strong>
        <span>Desktop + live rate limits</span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
      <a href="/providers/claude-code">
        <span class="provider-number mono">02</span>
        <strong>Claude Code</strong>
        <span>Isolated terminal / beta</span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
    </div>
    <figure class="dock-preview">
      <div class="media-label mono">
        <span>THE DOCK / DEVELOPMENT INTERFACE</span>
        <span>SAFE SAMPLE PROFILES</span>
      </div>
      <img
        src="/media/veydock-mixed-provider-dock.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="1000"
        alt="Actual VeyDock development interface with Codex and Claude Code beta profile cards, using clearly simulated data"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Actual interface, simulated data. Claude beta is in development. Check
        release notes for the capabilities of the current download.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>
  <section class="fit-section wrap" aria-labelledby="fit-title">
    <div class="fit-heading"><p class="eyebrow">IS THIS YOUR WORKFLOW?</p><h2 id="fit-title">Multiple accounts.<br /><span>One clear view.</span></h2><p>VeyDock is for Windows developers who want to see their accounts and allowances together, without losing track of the identity they chose.</p></div>
    <div class="fit-rows">
      <div><span class="mono">01 / KNOW</span><h3>See the limits before you launch.</h3><p>Compare provider-reported Codex usage windows and reset times. Timestamped cached readings stay clearly labelled when a refresh fails.</p><a href="/guides/codex-usage-limits-windows">Understand Codex usage limits ↗</a></div>
      <div><span class="mono">02 / CHOOSE</span><h3>Personal, work or reserved. Your choice.</h3><p>Name your authorised profiles, keep project shortcuts nearby and preserve Friend Priority. There is no fixed account cap or automatic preference for Pro.</p><a href="/guides/multiple-codex-accounts-windows">Manage multiple Codex accounts ↗</a></div>
      <div><span class="mono">03 / OWN</span><h3>Free to use. Open to inspect.</h3><p>No VeyDock subscription, paywall or account-count upgrade. Download the Windows app, inspect its source and adapt it under the MIT licence.</p><a href={repository}>Explore the open-source app ↗</a></div>
    </div>
    <p class="fit-boundary">VeyDock does not include a Codex or Claude subscription, increase provider limits or bypass their terms. It is an unsigned prerelease. Real account-switching acceptance is still pending. <a href="/providers">Check the provider capabilities before downloading ↗</a></p>
  </section>
  <SwitchFilm />
  <section class="editorial">
    <div class="wrap editorial-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">DIFFERENT TOOLS. ONE DELIBERATE CHOICE.</p>
      <h2>
        Choose the profile.
        <br />
        <span>Keep the context.</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="editorial-copy">
        <p>
          Personal work. Client work. A reserved account for a friend. They
          deserve names, boundaries and a place to land.
        </p>
        <p>
          VeyDock organises the profiles you authorise. It does not change
          provider quotas, promise unlimited usage or decide who should get
          priority.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/features">
          What the dock does ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="sequence" class="sequence wrap">
    <div class="sequence-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE DOCK RAIL</p>
      <p class="mono">01 → 05 / UNDER YOUR CONTROL</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sequence-grid">
      <nav class="rail" aria-label="Product sequence">
        {scenes.map(([n, label]) => (
          <a href={`#scene-${n}`} data-rail={n}>
            <span class="rail-dot"></span>
            <span>
              {n} / {label}
            </span>
          </a>
        ))}
      </nav>
      <div class="scene-copy">
        {scenes.map(([n, label, title, body, href]) => (
          <section class="scene" id={`scene-${n}`} data-scene={n}>
            <p class="eyebrow">
              {n} / {label}
            </p>
            <h2>
              {title.split("\n").map((line) => (
                <span>
                  {line}
                  <br />
                </span>
              ))}
            </h2>
            <p>{body}</p>
            {n === "05" ? (
              <button class="button secondary" data-command>
                Try quick dock <kbd>Ctrl K</kbd>
              </button>
            ) : (
              <a class="text-link" href={href}>
                Read the details ↗
              </a>
            )}
          </section>
        ))}
      </div>
      <figure class="sequence-media" data-focus="01">
        <div class="media-label mono">ACTUAL INTERFACE / SAMPLE DATA</div>
        <div class="screen-crop">
          <img
            src="/media/veydock-mixed-provider-dock.webp"
            width="1600"
            height="1000"
            loading="lazy"
            alt="Mixed provider profiles in the VeyDock development build"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption id="scene-caption">
          Configured profiles, without a fixed limit.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="provider-matrix wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">CAPABILITIES, NOT ASSUMPTIONS</p>
    <h2>
      Same dock.
      <br />
      <span>Provider-specific behaviour.</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <caption>
          Implemented development capabilities. Real account switching
          acceptance is pending.
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Capability</th>
            <th scope="col">Codex</th>
            <th scope="col">Claude Code beta</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Launch surface</th>
            <td>Existing Desktop workspace</td>
            <td>Isolated Windows terminal</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Usage source</th>
            <td>App-server rate limits</td>
            <td>Supported status-line fields</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Freshness</th>
            <td>Independent live refresh</td>
            <td>Last confirmed after a response</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Sign-in</th>
            <td>Normal OpenAI browser flow</td>
            <td>Normal claude.ai CLI flow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Isolation boundary</th>
            <td>Managed homes, shared Desktop handoff</td>
            <td>Separate CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Acceptance</th>
            <td>Desktop A/B/A pending</td>
            <td>Real A/B/A pending, beta</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="alpha-note">
      Claude keyless Console sign-ins are not isolated by its configuration
      directory and are unsupported in this beta. Concurrent-account reliability
      is not claimed.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/providers">
      Explore both providers ↗
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="trust wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">LOCAL CONTROL / OPEN SOURCE</p>
    <div class="trust-title">
      <h2>
        A utility you can
        <br />
        <span>look inside.</span>
      </h2>
      <p>
        MIT licensed. Created by Mithil Katkoria.
        <br />
        Independent of OpenAI and Anthropic.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="trust-rows">
      <a href="/security">
        <strong>Sign-in stays with the provider.</strong>
        <span>No password collection. No Claude credential copying.</span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
      <a href="/security#local-data">
        <strong>Local files are still sensitive.</strong>
        <span>
          Keep authentication out of Git and public support attachments.
        </span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
      <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/VERIFICATION.md`}>
        <strong>Evidence before promises.</strong>
        <span>
          Build checks and real account acceptance are different tests.
        </span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
      <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>
        <strong>MIT. Yours to inspect and adapt.</strong>
        <span>
          Keep the copyright and licence notice. Respect the branding.
        </span>
        <b>↗</b>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="product-questions wrap" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU DOWNLOAD</p><h2 id="questions-title">A few useful answers.</h2>
    <details><summary>Is VeyDock really free?</summary><p>Yes. The Windows app and its MIT-licensed source are free. There is no paid VeyDock plan or fixed profile limit. OpenAI and Anthropic accounts, subscriptions and usage charges remain separate.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does VeyDock do for multiple Codex accounts?</summary><p>It keeps named profiles, real reported usage limits, reset times, reservations and project launch targets in one Windows dashboard. You choose the profile explicitly. Read the <a href="/guides/switch-codex-accounts-windows">Codex switching guide</a> for the normal-quit handoff and current acceptance limitations.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does it support Claude Code?</summary><p>Yes, in beta. It prepares separate Claude Code configuration directories and launches the official native Windows CLI in a terminal. Supported quota readings are labelled Last confirmed. It does not switch Claude Desktop accounts. Real two-account isolation and persistent relaunch acceptance are pending. See <a href="/docs/providers/claude-code">Claude Code setup</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Will I need to give VeyDock my password?</summary><p>No. Connect through the provider's normal browser or CLI sign-in. Saved local authentication files can contain sensitive tokens, so protect your Windows account and do not share profile folders. Read <a href="/security">how authentication is handled</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do I need to install it?</summary><p>A Windows computer, WebView2 and the provider you want to use. The current installer is x64; Windows ARM uses x64 emulation. Codex uses Desktop and CLI. Claude Code beta uses its native Windows CLI. You do not need Node.js or Rust to run the compiled app. See <a href="/download">downloads and requirements</a>.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Is VeyDock an official OpenAI or Anthropic app?</summary><p>No. VeyDock is an independent community project by Mithil Katkoria. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by either provider. The <a href={repository}>public source</a>, <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/VERIFICATION.md`}>verification record</a> and <a href="/changelog">release history</a> are available to inspect.</p></details>
  </section>
  <section class="final-download wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">READY TO DOCK?</p>
    <div>
      <h2>
        Your profiles.
        <br />A clearer next move.
      </h2>
      <a class="button" href="/download">
        Get VeyDock for Windows <span>↓</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="mono">v{r.version} / WINDOWS X64 / UNSIGNED PRERELEASE</p>
    <p class="alpha-note">
      The current download is an unsigned prerelease. Windows may display an
      unknown-publisher warning. Provider support depends on the downloaded
      version. Real switching and installer-update acceptance remain pending.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
